00:04:21.225 --> 00:04:23.600
some hints about how to think
about pricing as it relates

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.660
to Firebase, how to do some sort
of forward-looking analysis.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.403
Just a little bit on that.

00:04:28.403 --> 00:04:31.070
And just some of the value props
that change when you're looking

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.820
at using something like
Firebase versus a more

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:37.670
closer-to-bare-metal stack
like Google Cloud or even

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.077
a fully-traditional
on-prem solution.

00:04:41.077 --> 00:04:42.660
So that's what we're
going to go over.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.110
We're going to just step
through those one at a time,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.318
so we're just going to dive
into the first one, which

00:04:47.318 --> 00:04:48.650
is clean, strong code.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.320
Now, if you are an existing
company using Firebase,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.242
there's a couple of situations
you might find yourself in.

00:04:54.242 --> 00:04:55.700
And generally
enterprise developers

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.530
find themselves in
one specific one,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.870
which is that you
have some product

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:03.170
and that product makes a lot
of money for your company,

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.060
probably, hopefully.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.300
And you have some
other part that

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.730
is the part that uses Firebase.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.840
This happens for
a lot of reasons,

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.900
but the biggest ones are that
Firebase is a great value add.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.010
So if you're a new team building
up something inside of a bigger

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.728
company, it lets you move fast.

00:05:19.728 --> 00:05:21.270
It lets you do things
really quickly.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.250
You can kind of get a minimum
viable thing out, and then

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.220
iterate on it.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.550
And we see a lot of products
like that using Firebase.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.010
And then the other type of
enterprise using Firebase

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:33.070
is any enterprise that's
actually relying on Firebase

00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:35.800
as a core part of its business.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.673
This is extremely rare.

00:05:37.673 --> 00:05:39.340
And this is one of
the reasons that it's

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.010
important Firebase is as
connected to Google Cloud

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:42.720
as it is today.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.690
We want to make sure that as
you grow and build with Firebase

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.040
there is a clear connection
between Firebase and Google

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:48.760
Cloud.

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.340
So if you're using Google
Cloud or you're using Firebase

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:53.768
and you realize you need
something from the other one,

00:05:53.768 --> 00:05:55.060
they're not mutually exclusive.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.500
They're designed
to work together

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.600
and to kind of help each other.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.940
But in either of
these cases, if you're

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.690
using Firebase
somewhere in your stack,

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:07.690
you've probably realized
that the code you're writing

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.490
is a little sloppy.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:11.060
This happens all the time.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.080
It's just kind of part
of building things today.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.600
And I would argue
that it's not just

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:18.850
a side-effect of writing code.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.380
I think there's a
specific culture

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:22.450
that working with
Firebase lends itself to,

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:24.640
which is the culture building
quickly and iterating

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.150
on things, that also lends
itself to writing bad code.

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.960
And that's what I
call the MVP culture,

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.460
or that Minimum Viable
Product culture.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:36.760
And this is a bit of a tangent.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:37.520
I'll give you that.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:38.860
But I think it's a
really interesting one

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:40.235
to think about--
where, if you're

00:06:40.235 --> 00:06:42.310
building an MVP inside
of your enterprise, which

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:45.580
is, as I said, what a lot
of Firebase developers do,

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:49.390
then you have to wonder what
is changing about this product

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:50.200
as I'm building it.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:50.500
Right?

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:52.958
You're like, all right, these
features are going to change.

00:06:52.958 --> 00:06:56.200
I'm going to figure out what
users want and respond to that.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:58.700
But in that churn what
happens to the code?

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.200
Right?

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.368
The code gets mangled.

00:07:00.368 --> 00:07:02.410
You're like, oh, there
was this whole other thing

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.580
we built we don't need,
and then we drop that off.

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:05.933
We don't do anything with it.

00:07:05.933 --> 00:07:07.100
We built up this other part.

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.490
Maybe the entire original
purpose of the code base

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:10.630
gets shifted.

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:13.030
All sorts of crazy
things happen.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:16.330
And that's fine, because
when we're building an MVP

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.490
we're writing the
minimum amount of code.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.887
We're doing so in
a way that allows

00:07:20.887 --> 00:07:22.720
us to, because the
iteration time is faster.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:24.970
We're not spending
a ton of time,

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:29.380
like, declaring a SOAP API
or something crazy like that.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.400
We're just like, eh,
throw up some JavaScript.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.030
Throw up a serverless
function that can do this.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:36.310
And-- there's a mouse.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.150
Pretend that didn't happen.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.590
And all of these
things are fine.

00:07:41.590 --> 00:07:44.350
But the result in
this-- the end result

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.570
is not just a better product
at the end of the day,

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:49.060
but it's actually
pretty expensive

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.270
because you're iterating on
this code base very quickly.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:53.770
You're changing things.

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:56.550
And as you do that, you're going
to allow your developers more

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.300
time to go back and
rewrite the same thing

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.680
and rewrite the same
thing, and you build up

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:00.847
a lot of code debt this way.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:02.920
You build up a lot
of issues that you're

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:05.080
going to have to deal
with down the road.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.610
And this is part
of this MVP culture

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.110
that people don't
really talk about.

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:11.335
And I see this particularly in
enterprises trying to spin up

00:08:11.335 --> 00:08:12.460
things quickly in Firebase.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.377
Because you're always
under crazy constraints,

00:08:14.377 --> 00:08:17.200
and you always have something
that has to be done next week.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.090
And they build like this.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.420
So when we talk
about MVPs, when we

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.730
talk about this idea
of iterating quickly,

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:30.075
there is one friendly
language that always comes up.

00:08:30.075 --> 00:08:30.700
It is this one.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.350
It's JavaScript.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.608
If you have ever written
anything with Firebase,

00:08:34.608 --> 00:08:36.650
you've probably touched
JavaScript at some point,

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.740
whether it's using one
of our JavaScript SDKs

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.500
or using the Cloud Functions,
which most people write

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:42.789
in JavaScript.

00:08:42.789 --> 00:08:46.710
But despite the
better iteration time,

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.070
despite JavaScript's ability to
allow you to write code really

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:52.860
fast and spin stuff up and
really quickly get something

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.640
together, it has a
ton of downsides.

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:57.390
And some of you may
be thinking, of course

00:08:57.390 --> 00:08:59.730
I know JavaScript is not
the perfect language,

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.920
but I really want to
stress this as it relates

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.230
to Firebase because a
huge number of developers

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:06.510
don't understand that if
you're using something

00:09:06.510 --> 00:09:08.070
like Cloud Functions
for Firebase

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.920
or any of our other services,
you are definitely not

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.380
locked into JavaScript.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.910
JavaScript makes sense
in some situations,

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.690
but in others it doesn't.

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.890
And so here's an example,
a snippet of JavaScript.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.110
This is some code that
interacts with Firebase.

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:25.590
It's just, like, a call to the
database, checks if a user has

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:27.970
an admin role, returns a value.

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:31.830
And if you are a really keen
JavaScript developer or even

00:09:31.830 --> 00:09:33.600
a pretty keen
JavaScript developer,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:36.290
you might realize that this
snippet makes no sense.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:36.790
All right?

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:40.110
But if you are anything but
a pretty keen JavaScript

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.300
developer, this
probably looked fine.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.270
And this is exactly the issue
with JavaScript as it grows

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:46.830
and as you work
with bigger teams

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:48.920
and grow your product
and things like that.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:52.080
A good developer-- a real good
developer can see the problem

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.420
here, but an average
developer can't.

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:55.950
A new developer can't.

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:59.130
A developer who is not familiar
with Firebase or these SDKs

00:09:59.130 --> 00:09:59.950
can't.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:00.450
All right?

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.330
So here's the cheat sheet
of what's wrong with this.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:05.800
Obviously firebase.database
is a function,

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.280
so you're missing
your invocation there.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:09.780
How could you not notice that?

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:10.740
We're missing a return.

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.610
This value never actually
gets out anywhere.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:13.652
We don't have a callback.

00:10:13.652 --> 00:10:15.120
We don't return the promise.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.590
And, of course,
that string, val,

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:19.620
is actually supposed
to be value.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:20.690
All these are trivial.

00:10:20.690 --> 00:10:21.190
Right?

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:22.110
They're a couple of characters.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:23.730
They're an easy
mistake to make, even

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.100
if you're familiar
with JavaScript,

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:27.957
with the Firebase SDK.

00:10:27.957 --> 00:10:29.540
But if you are not
that person, if you

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:31.650
are anyone else
coming into this team,

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.290
you're probably
going to miss this.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:37.020
So this leads me
to a general idea

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:40.050
I have about choosing
languages and choosing

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.880
what to build your
product in and what

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.950
to build with in
Firebase, which is

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.530
that the language
your code is in

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.630
should be as strict as
the contracts provided

00:10:48.630 --> 00:10:50.730
by the services
it interacts with.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:53.250
This is kind of a weird mantra.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.460
And it basically is saying,
don't use JavaScript.

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.720
It's probably the wrong idea.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.470
It's a little more
formal than that so you

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.720
can tell it to people and
they think you're smart.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.030
But let me explain
what I mean here.

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:08.280
So imagine you have a big
micros service architecture.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.822
Maybe it's a serverless
architecture.

00:11:09.822 --> 00:11:11.530
You got a bunch of
functions around.

00:11:11.530 --> 00:11:12.200
That's great.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:12.840
Right?

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.320
You have some off-service
that a bunch of other services

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:16.830
rely on.

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.590
You have ordering and scheduling
and tracking and reporting.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:21.390
And that core service
provides an API

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:23.550
that all of these
services relate to.

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:26.502
In reality, there's
a chance at first

00:11:26.502 --> 00:11:28.710
that the ordering and the
scheduling and the tracking

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:29.970
and the reporting
and the auth are all

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.678
maintained by the same
team, but once you

00:11:31.678 --> 00:11:34.030
grow, once you get a
few auth providers,

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:37.392
once you get a bigger
and bigger team,

00:11:37.392 --> 00:11:39.600
you're going to get a bunch
of these that are all now

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.240
providing different APIs,
that all of these services

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:45.270
are now expected to know
how to interact with.

00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:49.500
And this interaction
here, that JSON bit,

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.190
is a very loose, very,
very soft format.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.420
You can send back
anything you want,

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.880
and the other service is
expected to handle it.

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:00.755
So if, for example, our social
auth, our Facebook auth,

00:12:00.755 --> 00:12:03.738
and our Twitter auth hands back
an object to these services.

00:12:03.738 --> 00:12:04.530
That looks one way.

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:06.708
Maybe it has display
underscore name.

00:12:06.708 --> 00:12:09.000
But our internal auth-- that
hands back something else.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.760
That hands back something
that's called display Name,

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:15.330
with a capital "N", camel case.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:18.810
So we get into this issue
where now these core services

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.270
are providing different
APIs, different services,

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:22.560
to all these dependents.

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:24.540
And now each of
these dependents is

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:27.142
responsible for handling
the different cases,

00:12:27.142 --> 00:12:29.100
the different situations
that could be provided

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:31.650
by this very loose API.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:33.900
And in this case
JavaScript might

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.952
make sense, because if
all of these services

00:12:35.952 --> 00:12:37.410
are providing
different things, you

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.720
don't have a strong contract
between your different

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.960
services, between your service
functions and your client side.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.850
If you don't have
that guarantee,

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.560
you want a very
flexible language.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.150
You want a language
like JavaScript

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.280
that can take
whatever you give it

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:54.270
and probably mangle it
into something usable.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.910
But we don't want to get in this
situation in the first place,

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.270
because this doesn't scale.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:12:58.770
Right?

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:01.470
Like, as soon as you start doing
this a bunch and you have not

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:04.093
only an auth service
or a few auth services,

00:13:04.093 --> 00:13:05.760
maintained perhaps
by different people--

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.302
at least some were maintained
by people from three years ago,

00:13:08.302 --> 00:13:10.460
some are working there
now, some don't know--

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:13.030
you're going to hit this issue.

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:17.210
So we have this snippet
with a bunch of issues.

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:17.970
Right?

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:21.030
And of course we're
thinking, well, we

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:22.710
aren't talking about JSON here.

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:24.960
We're not talking
about any interaction

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.980
here that is nice and
soft and loose like JSON.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.560
We have a really
strong contract here.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.970
We have an SDK.

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:36.690
We have a nice, nice
documented, clean, consistent,

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.240
properly-typed, beautiful SDK--

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.660
Firebase SDK here, and we're
just throwing that all away.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.990
And we're just like, we don't
care that this does not--

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:50.160
this might be able to tell me
that database is a function.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:53.070
We don't care that it knows
it's supposed to take value.

00:13:53.070 --> 00:13:55.230
We just toss this out.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.162
And this is kind of
the core realization

00:13:57.162 --> 00:13:58.620
here that I'd like
you to take away

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:01.560
from this bit, which is that
these SDKs that we provide,

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.810
that Google Cloud provides,
that Firebase provides--

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:06.420
these are contracts that
we are making to you.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:08.160
Obviously not legal contracts.

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:10.720
Not something that you
can sue us if we change.

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:12.840
But they are the
type of thing that we

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.420
do our absolute best not to
mess with you and not to change.

00:14:16.420 --> 00:14:20.040
So in this situation where we
have all of this information,

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.960
throwing it away,
using it in JavaScript

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.640
makes a lot less sense.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:26.350
So this SDK is a
strong contract.

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:26.850
Right?

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:29.250
We're communicating
between these services--

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:31.740
in this case Firebase
services, but in this example

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:35.250
it's an internal app
communicating to a dependent.

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.490
Strongly-typed languages
make more sense.

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.140
Because there is
nothing loose here.

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:43.110
There is nothing flexible
about this contract

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:44.347
from another service.

00:14:44.347 --> 00:14:46.680
So if we look at this snippet
again we think, all right.

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:48.880
Why are we relying
on a language that

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:50.130
lets us have that flexibility?

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:51.700
It really doesn't
gain us that much.

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:53.738
In fact, it makes
the situation worse

00:14:53.738 --> 00:14:55.530
because now developers
are making mistakes.

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:56.765
They have to iterate more.

00:14:56.765 --> 00:14:58.890
They have to come back and,
as things change, which

00:14:58.890 --> 00:15:03.180
they inevitably will, they are
more likely to break this code.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:07.050
So here's the same
function rewritten

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:10.590
into a typed language.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:12.988
It looks almost
identical, doesn't it?

00:15:12.988 --> 00:15:13.530
I mean, yeah.

00:15:13.530 --> 00:15:16.150
It's pretty much-- it's
slightly different.

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:20.280
It honestly just looks like
more modern JavaScript.

00:15:20.280 --> 00:15:23.670
But introducing some
TypeScript into the situation

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:26.170
changes the world for us.

00:15:26.170 --> 00:15:27.870
This is now a valid
TypeScript snippet.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.120
And although we haven't
put any types on here--

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:34.740
we don't say uid is a string, we
don't promise that we're going

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:37.990
to return a value or
anything like that--

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:41.752
we are making use of the
contract provided by Firebase.

00:15:41.752 --> 00:15:43.710
Because if we tried to
compile this and turn it

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:47.790
into JavaScript and we had any
of those mistakes back here--

00:15:47.790 --> 00:15:48.900
excuse me.

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:50.820
If we had any of the
mistakes back here,

00:15:50.820 --> 00:15:52.190
TypeScript would tell us.

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:52.878
Right?

00:15:52.878 --> 00:15:54.420
So we're making a
very small change--

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.378
like, a few characters.

00:15:55.378 --> 00:15:57.990
But now the end result
is that our code

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.970
is making use of that contract.

00:15:59.970 --> 00:16:02.092
It's saying, I know you're
giving me this data,

00:16:02.092 --> 00:16:04.050
you're giving me these
fields and these objects

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:05.250
and these things like that.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.920
Well, let's actually
listen and obey those.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.210
So just this switch to
TypeScript-- very low cost

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:13.920
in general, especially
for new projects.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.170
If you're building
something up old

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.628
and you're trying
to transition it,

00:16:16.628 --> 00:16:18.128
it can be pretty
expensive to shift.

00:16:18.128 --> 00:16:19.670
But if you're building
something new,

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:21.180
you're trying to
MVP something, this

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:23.460
will save you time-- just
switching to TypeScript.

00:16:23.460 --> 00:16:25.200
I love TypeScript,
but my whole talk

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:26.760
is not just about TypeScript.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.970
But one last part that is about
TypeScript is code completion.

00:16:29.970 --> 00:16:32.640
I don't know if you've ever
used JavaScript code completion

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.860
or if you've ever seen a
new developer on your team

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.140
try to figure out what
the hell is going on

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.150
in your JavaScript--
but they're not

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:40.710
going to get
anything this pretty.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.160
They're not going to
get anything that says,

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:44.460
here are the methods that
are actually on this SDK.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:46.470
They're going to get
a whole bunch of junk

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:49.762
that, like, Sublime thinks
it can read and realize,

00:16:49.762 --> 00:16:51.720
because it's got some
crazy regx and it's like,

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.312
this is probably a JavaScript
value, but it's not good.

00:16:54.312 --> 00:16:55.020
It's not helpful.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:56.950
And, frankly, it
can be misleading.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.820
So if we use a stronger type
language, not only have we

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:04.109
now enabled our systems to use
and consume those contracts,

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:07.807
but we're improving
the experience

00:17:07.807 --> 00:17:08.849
for these new developers.

00:17:08.849 --> 00:17:10.470
They're coming in and
they're seeing this

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:11.329
and they're saying, all right.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:12.089
I can figure this out.

00:17:12.089 --> 00:17:13.500
I can reason about this better.

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.349
I can get up to speed quicker,
and I can make changes more

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:18.400
confidently.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.300
So one last bit about this
whole service contract language

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:22.890
decision is that I
want to point out

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:25.980
that services are anything
a team relies on which isn't

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:28.470
written by that same team.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.300
So when I say
"services," I don't just

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:31.890
mean things like Google Cloud.

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.480
I don't mean things
like Firebase.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:36.270
It can be, like the earlier
example, your auth service.

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:39.180
There is no reason your auth
service couldn't provide

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:42.410
an SDK with that really
nice, strict contract

00:17:42.410 --> 00:17:45.240
that they maintain and they
give to the other teams and say,

00:17:45.240 --> 00:17:47.140
you don't have to talk
over our JSON API.

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:49.150
If that API changes,
we'll take care of it.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.720
But we maintain both the core
service and the associated SDK,

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.580
which is what you actually
use to talk to our service.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:57.990
And now if they make
a change in their SDK

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:00.180
because they made a change
on their server code,

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:02.187
you can now respond
to that reasonably.

00:18:02.187 --> 00:18:03.270
Can get build time errors.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:04.920
You can get more
information than you

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:08.640
would if that SDK
or that situation

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.950
was more loosely-typed.

00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:12.990
The other thing that
counts as a service is,

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:17.080
every single one of those node
modules you've ever pulled in--

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:18.630
for example, if I
went and I tried

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:23.700
to find a colors module to just
throw some color in my logging

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:24.910
output--

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:27.330
I don't know if you know
this, but there's 3,600 color

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.610
modules, which is ridiculous.

00:18:29.610 --> 00:18:31.137
A lot of them are
not what I want,

00:18:31.137 --> 00:18:33.720
but if I didn't know what I want
I'd be able to kind of reduce

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:35.460
it down to two these two.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:38.070
And when I go and I
take on a dependency

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:40.392
from a third party like
this, I'm making an agreement

00:18:40.392 --> 00:18:41.850
with that person
that's saying, I'm

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.370
trusting you on a
bunch of levels.

00:18:44.370 --> 00:18:47.390
I'm trusting you not to do
anything evil in my code,

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:49.140
because once I'm
importing your JavaScript

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:50.723
you can do whatever
the heck you want.

00:18:50.723 --> 00:18:51.740
You can break my page.

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.260
You can steal my information.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:55.620
So you have that level
of contract and agreement

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:56.410
with them.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.040
But you're also making a
technical agreement saying,

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.520
please don't change this
API too much, because if you

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:04.050
do I'm not going to know.

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:06.580
Like, it's just going to break
in production at some point

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:08.340
if they change how
these APIs work.

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:10.860
And some developers are much
more reasonable and much more

00:19:10.860 --> 00:19:13.140
considerate, and
they're willing to make

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:17.700
a guarantee of some form
with you that their API is

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:19.357
going to be a certain way.

00:19:19.357 --> 00:19:20.940
And we can look at
these two packages.

00:19:20.940 --> 00:19:23.250
If we were to look at these
without being a JavaScript

00:19:23.250 --> 00:19:24.930
developer, or being a
new JavaScript developer

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:26.930
or what have you, it would
be impossible to tell

00:19:26.930 --> 00:19:29.650
which of these is
the ideal one to use.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.750
You might know
offhand, but we can go

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:34.370
and we can look into these
repos and we can check out

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:37.290
the package.json
file for colors,

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.180
and you'll notice
through all this

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:42.360
there is no mentions of
types or any guarantee

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:45.630
that what we're outputting
here is going to stay the same.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:48.130
And this is the
non-TypeScript library.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:51.350
It's usable, but
it's also deprecated.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.850
It's several years old, and
it's definitely not the choice

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:54.780
you want to make.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.928
This one, "chalk,"
on the other hand--

00:19:56.928 --> 00:19:58.470
you can see right
there under "files"

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:01.490
there's a type/index.d.ts.

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:04.200
And that's saying, hey,
here's the actual file

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.870
that defines the contract
I'm providing you

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:08.560
what your service can rely on.

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:12.270
So if this changes, if that DTS
file changes on a new release,

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.760
that'll be a build time error
instead of a runtime error.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:18.390
And that's a better sign of
a developer willing to make

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:22.860
a commitment to you,
upholding some bar of quality,

00:20:22.860 --> 00:20:24.920
than the ones who don't.

00:20:24.920 --> 00:20:28.030
So this is a yay TypeScript
kind of situation.

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:31.020
And I generally recommend, even
if you're not using TypeScript,

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:32.880
look for libraries
that support TypeScript

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.580
because it's a better indicator
that people are willing to be

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.490
consistent and they're not just
changing things willy-nilly

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:39.430
under you.

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:42.120
This doesn't help at all if this
person is malicious, obviously.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.310
They can still steal
all your information.

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.740
But at least your code's
not going to change.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:50.460
So with all these thousands
of trust relationships

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:53.550
we have with external
people, ideally

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:54.780
they would all be contracts.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:54.960
Right?

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:57.030
Like, every single one
of these we would say,

00:20:57.030 --> 00:20:59.047
you were providing
me this type of SDK.

00:20:59.047 --> 00:21:00.755
You were providing me
this cloud service,

00:21:00.755 --> 00:21:02.250
this whatever, whatever.

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:05.022
And you would have this very,
very nice, clean structure.

00:21:05.022 --> 00:21:07.230
But there are certain
languages which just don't lend

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:08.950
themselves well to doing this.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:10.540
JavaScript is one of those.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:12.420
So if the language is
weak, because that's

00:21:12.420 --> 00:21:15.990
the contract between the
provider and the dependent,

00:21:15.990 --> 00:21:18.240
your entire service
is going to be weak.

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:20.780
If your language is strong,
it's going to be stronger.

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:23.670
So any strictly-typed language--

00:21:23.670 --> 00:21:26.790
Go, for example,
Java, for example--

00:21:26.790 --> 00:21:28.323
are great choices here.

00:21:28.323 --> 00:21:29.740
And luckily you
might be thinking,

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.710
well, this is all great, but
Firebase, Cloud Functions--

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:35.280
they only support JavaScript.

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:37.980
Maybe they support TypeScript,
but it's not really that--

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:39.420
which is not true.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.235
You can write Go code and deploy
it up to Cloud Functions today.

00:21:42.235 --> 00:21:44.110
You can use all of the
same triggers you use.

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:45.770
You can use all the
same stuff, and it's

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:46.895
going to work just as well.

00:21:46.895 --> 00:21:48.090
We have Go SDKs.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.690
We have SDKs from many
strictly-typed languages

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:52.510
on the back-end.

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.900
We have a lot of choices
here that aren't JavaScript

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:57.720
that still allow you flexibility
and speed of development,

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.410
but with nice, strict contracts
that we're agreeing to,

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:03.948
to make sure that what
we give you is either

00:22:03.948 --> 00:22:05.490
going to be the same
as it was before

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:08.110
or it's going to
fail at build time.

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:09.827
So that's my whole
spiel about languages.

00:22:09.827 --> 00:22:10.910
Don't write in JavaScript.

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:11.743
Write in TypeScript.

00:22:11.743 --> 00:22:14.530
Or even better yet, write in
Go or something like that.

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:16.320
But we got a lot else
to cover, and that

00:22:16.320 --> 00:22:17.800
was a pretty long section.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:21.300
So I want to talk about
systematic testing.

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.130
So the first thing
about testing,

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.410
if you're writing a
big team-- and this

00:22:25.410 --> 00:22:27.150
goes back to that
MVP culture thing.

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:27.650
Right?

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:31.620
Like, MVPs generally don't
have tests, which is terrible.

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:33.120
But one of the
reasons they don't is

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.370
because testing is pretty hard.

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:37.258
It's a very difficult
thing to get right.

00:22:37.258 --> 00:22:38.800
And if you're trying
to move quickly,

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:40.842
a lot of times "move fast
and break things" means

00:22:40.842 --> 00:22:43.240
"break things because
we didn't write tests."

00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:45.990
And we need to do what
we can, culturally,

00:22:45.990 --> 00:22:49.020
when we're building things, to
avoid getting into situations

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:50.528
where it's hard to test things.

00:22:50.528 --> 00:22:53.070
So here's an example, another
snippet, another Firebase code,

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:54.510
more JavaScript.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.375
And it's, unlike the
other JavaScript,

00:22:57.375 --> 00:22:58.250
a little more recent.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:22:59.170
It's got some async/await.

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:00.253
It's got some other stuff.

00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:06.420
The problem with this code
is that the only thing

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.940
this JavaScript
module is exporting

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:11.260
is this Cloud
Function definition.

00:23:11.260 --> 00:23:13.080
So we can deploy
this to Firebase,

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:14.730
and once it's in
production at Firebase

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:17.490
we can hit it and see what
happens and we test it,

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:19.870
but that is incredibly slow.

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:23.910
It's like a minute at least
of loop for you to go,

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:25.890
I'm going to change
this, deploy it, test it.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:28.140
And worse than that, you need
to be deploying it constantly

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:29.460
to our staging
environment, which you have

00:23:29.460 --> 00:23:31.000
to have in the first place.

00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.520
And it's very, very tedious.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:36.030
So if we get into a situation
where instead of building out

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.430
this big method that
whatever it does it

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:41.550
sends messages or push
notifications to people,

00:23:41.550 --> 00:23:43.770
we restructure it.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:46.380
And we expose that same
business logic in multiple ways

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:48.190
that is now independent
of the vendor.

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.400
So this is code, and this
is an example of me saying,

00:23:50.400 --> 00:23:53.718
don't do it the Firebase way if
you can because all the testing

00:23:53.718 --> 00:23:56.010
tools, all the unit tools,
all the spies and everything

00:23:56.010 --> 00:23:58.302
like that in the world, are
not going to make it easier

00:23:58.302 --> 00:24:00.180
for you to test your
code than if you just

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:02.670
export a normal method
that you can just tie into

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:03.687
and check the result of.

00:24:03.687 --> 00:24:05.020
So that's what we're doing here.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:07.172
We're taking this
old big blob of code

00:24:07.172 --> 00:24:09.630
and moving all this business
logic, all the part that you'd

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:11.220
actually want to
test, throwing it

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.200
into a new method, which
we call "send to room."

00:24:14.200 --> 00:24:16.470
And then after that,
instead of just exposing

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:19.310
that one Cloud Function,
that one Firestore trigger,

00:24:19.310 --> 00:24:21.310
we're going to expose a
couple different things.

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:23.185
We're going to expose
a manual trigger, which

00:24:23.185 --> 00:24:24.660
is an HTTPS function.

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:27.055
This we can emulate
locally, so it's very easy

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:28.680
to hit and get a
response from, and see

00:24:28.680 --> 00:24:31.890
if send to room with data
is working as we'd expect.

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:34.237
We now have the
automatic room, which

00:24:34.237 --> 00:24:35.820
is an automatic send
to room function.

00:24:35.820 --> 00:24:37.590
This is exactly
like it was before.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.410
And you can see any
logic that's related

00:24:40.410 --> 00:24:42.630
to that Firestore trigger
is going to be tied

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:44.980
in in this little bit of code.

00:24:44.980 --> 00:24:46.582
So we're pulling in
from text, roomId,

00:24:46.582 --> 00:24:48.540
and passing that to send
to room, which is just

00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:50.130
a normal JavaScript function.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:52.020
So just by reorganizing
things a little bit

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:55.260
and always having a manual
way to trigger things,

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:58.020
we're helping avoid the
situation where a developer is

00:24:58.020 --> 00:25:01.740
going to go and have to
deploy something to test it,

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:05.800
and increase that time to loop.

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:08.170
Here's everything we did
that makes this better.

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:09.817
We export a top-level
namespace now,

00:25:09.817 --> 00:25:11.400
which you can do
with Cloud Functions.

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.440
You can import, like, an
infinitely-deep object

00:25:13.440 --> 00:25:15.880
and it'll automatically
namespace things for you.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:19.360
So when you deploy this you'll
get fcm underscore manual.

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:21.450
You'll get fcm
underscore automatic,

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:23.730
underscore dash send to room.

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:24.540
So we do that.

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:25.920
We have that HTTPS function.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:28.680
We have function-specific
logic handled

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.080
all in the actual
trigger invocation,

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:33.460
and the business logic
pops somewhere else.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:35.260
Now, this is great.

00:25:35.260 --> 00:25:37.350
This is a helpful way
to organize your code,

00:25:37.350 --> 00:25:39.840
but it's not as
good as just being

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:43.460
able to invoke this code as it
is, the full production code

00:25:43.460 --> 00:25:43.968
naturally.

00:25:43.968 --> 00:25:45.510
Which, I have some
good news for you.

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.730
I wish I could tell you
more, but we are working

00:25:47.730 --> 00:25:50.250
on new Firebase emulators.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.590
We're working on an entire
suite of new emulators

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:55.650
that will allow you to do
entirely offline development

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:58.780
without deploying
to Firebase at all.

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:01.890
So this will make a lot of
things moot in this talk,

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:05.430
actually, but we're not
we're not there yet.

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:07.170
But once we're
getting there, we're

00:26:07.170 --> 00:26:09.330
going to have a much,
much better story for you

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:11.800
in terms of testing
and things like that.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:14.070
So in the meantime, keep
listening to what I'm saying,

00:26:14.070 --> 00:26:16.200
but keep a lookout
for the new emulators

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:17.520
that are coming down the pipe.

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:19.380
It's been far too
long that we have not

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:24.930
had a good offline story for
Firebase, and that is coming.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.660
Beyond that, testing
can't be optional.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:29.400
If anything is
remotely difficult

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:31.530
and you give someone
the option not to do it,

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:33.940
they probably won't do it.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:37.300
So we've got to make sure
they test their code.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.150
We can kind of make
sure they write tests.

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:39.340
Right?

00:26:39.340 --> 00:26:40.650
Like, we can have
code reviews and we

00:26:40.650 --> 00:26:42.608
can have things like that
that ensure coverage.

00:26:42.608 --> 00:26:44.580
We can use tools like
coveralls to make sure

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:47.320
that a good percentage of
our code is covered by tests.

00:26:47.320 --> 00:26:48.732
But we also have
all these tools.

00:26:48.732 --> 00:26:50.190
This text is a
little small, but we

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:52.590
have a lot of ways
to automatically deal

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:56.280
with running tests that
enforce developers use tests

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:58.947
all the time.

00:26:58.947 --> 00:27:01.530
For example, just to name a few,
you can use Git Commit Hooks.

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:04.530
So if somebody commits
code, it has to pass tests

00:27:04.530 --> 00:27:06.930
or it has to have a certain
coveralls percentage.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:09.490
We can do Automated Branch
Reviews from bots and things

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:10.450
like GitHub.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:12.870
There's Hot-reloadable Tests,
which make it much more

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:14.587
approachable to rerun tests.

00:27:14.587 --> 00:27:16.170
We have all sorts
of different things.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:17.040
Team-provided Mocks.

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:20.040
This goes back to the idea that
if your service is providing

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:22.890
an SDK, that SDK
should also have mocks

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:24.760
provided by that same team.

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:27.120
We can have code hygiene
practices, CI/CD stuff,

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:29.040
we can have hooks in
the Firebase CLI--

00:27:29.040 --> 00:27:31.860
all sorts of stuff that
enforce these tests be

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.290
ran by our developers.

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:36.540
So regardless of who
this developer is,

00:27:36.540 --> 00:27:38.963
they're not getting code in
unless they run the tests.

00:27:38.963 --> 00:27:41.130
And this is all just making
it part of the workflow.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:41.630
Right?

00:27:41.630 --> 00:27:43.955
We just want developers
to always be thinking,

00:27:43.955 --> 00:27:45.330
I'm going to write
test for this.

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.100
Tests are my default
way of testing this.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:48.570
I'm not going to click
through and click the buttons

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:49.260
and see if it works.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:50.968
I'm going to write a
test, because that's

00:27:50.968 --> 00:27:52.447
going to be better.

00:27:52.447 --> 00:27:54.780
I want to talk specifically
a bit more about this, which

00:27:54.780 --> 00:27:57.540
is leading into our next
section about deployments

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:58.947
and rollbacks.

00:27:58.947 --> 00:28:01.530
Some of you have probably heard
about this really cool service

00:28:01.530 --> 00:28:02.560
we have called Cloud Build.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:04.352
And if you haven't
heard about Cloud Build,

00:28:04.352 --> 00:28:06.930
you've probably heard about one
of our competitors' products.

00:28:06.930 --> 00:28:09.542
There's tons of good
CI/CD options out here,

00:28:09.542 --> 00:28:11.250
and this is one of
those situations where

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:13.500
I don't particularly care if
you use Cloud Build or you use

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:14.460
any of the other ones.

00:28:14.460 --> 00:28:15.450
Use the one that's best for you.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:17.620
Cloud Build is sweet because it
integrates with Google Cloud,

00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:19.590
so if your code's
already in Google Cloud

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:21.450
and your life is
already in Google Cloud,

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:22.890
you might as well
use Cloud Build.

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:24.765
But there's a lot of
great options out there.

00:28:24.765 --> 00:28:27.810
A lot of good documentation,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:30.180
To give you some idea about
what Cloud Build offers

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:33.480
in the simplest situation--
if you've never used CI/CD,

00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:35.610
your team is still
either manually

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.430
moving files to a
server or your team

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:43.290
is relying on the one dude who
remembers how to deploy code.

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.390
Like, he's the one with
the right shell scripts.

00:28:45.390 --> 00:28:46.740
This is for you.

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.980
If your team already has
CI/CD stuff, that's fine.

00:28:48.980 --> 00:28:51.180
You can tune out for a second.

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:52.895
So Cloud Builder is
super approachable.

00:28:52.895 --> 00:28:55.020
Continuous integration,
continuous deployment tool.

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:56.437
If you don't know
what that means,

00:28:56.437 --> 00:28:59.910
that means when you commit to
Git or you hit some button,

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:01.800
it builds your code,
it tests your code,

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:04.770
and it ships your code either
to production and staging, what

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:06.630
have you.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.330
It's a bit hard to see
in this tiny screenshot,

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:11.790
but the general idea here
is that every time I've

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:14.250
done anything to this code
base it runs these tests.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:15.810
You see a bunch of them
are failing, because I

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:16.935
wrote a bunch of bad tests.

00:29:16.935 --> 00:29:17.940
But that's fine.

00:29:17.940 --> 00:29:19.855
It's only trying
to go to staging.

00:29:19.855 --> 00:29:21.480
[LAUGHS] It's only
going to production.

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:23.017
It's only going to staging.

00:29:23.017 --> 00:29:25.350
So if the build fails, it
doesn't matter if it succeeds.

00:29:25.350 --> 00:29:25.850
Great.

00:29:25.850 --> 00:29:27.040
I can go check it.

00:29:27.040 --> 00:29:28.950
And this is kind
of the magic here,

00:29:28.950 --> 00:29:31.200
is that all of the process
that you would normally

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:36.047
do to get something into any
environment is contained here.

00:29:36.047 --> 00:29:38.130
And it's replicate-able
because all you have to do

00:29:38.130 --> 00:29:41.850
is commit to Git or do whatever,
and that's a very easy way

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:43.650
to get something out.

00:29:43.650 --> 00:29:46.680
The actual setup for
this is super trivial.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:48.960
Again, this is probably a
bit hard to read, sadly,

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.745
but you just make one of these
wonderful Cloud Build YAML

00:29:51.745 --> 00:29:52.245
files.

00:29:52.245 --> 00:29:55.900
They can pull in any arbitrary
Docker file, and that can run.

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:58.290
So we have G Cloud,
CLI, Docker files.

00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:00.210
We have Firebase
CLI Docker files.

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:03.913
We have all sorts of
node and any static site

00:30:03.913 --> 00:30:05.830
builder you would want,
or anything like that.

00:30:05.830 --> 00:30:09.480
You can just pull in as a step
in this, and then it'll go.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:12.300
And every time you
commit it will go and run

00:30:12.300 --> 00:30:14.380
this and deploy it up.

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:16.440
So here's an example
of a situation

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:18.407
where I've synced it to Git.

00:30:18.407 --> 00:30:19.740
We have a bunch of options here.

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.780
You can use the Cloud
Source repositories, which

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:23.550
are the Git repos
inside of Google

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:25.320
Cloud that most people
don't know about

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:26.650
but are super helpful.

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:29.010
We also actually have an
editor built into Google Cloud.

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:30.760
If you've never used
that, check that out.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:32.718
But if you're already on
GitHub, you're already

00:30:32.718 --> 00:30:34.710
on it Bitbucket,
whatever-- you can also

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:36.300
use a bunch of other
places, I think.

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:37.000
That's fine.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:40.500
You can just say, hey, anytime
I commit to GitHub hook this in,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:41.560
run this code.

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:43.523
So in this case
I've been working

00:30:43.523 --> 00:30:44.690
on whatever service this is.

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:47.280
It's clearly a very
good commit log.

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:49.410
I'm a very good developer.

00:30:49.410 --> 00:30:52.520
And imagine that last
commit I made a mistake.

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:53.220
Right?

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:55.305
I have accidentally
broken something.

00:30:55.305 --> 00:30:56.430
It's failing in production.

00:30:56.430 --> 00:30:58.320
Somehow it got past my tests.

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.690
Somehow we got in
this situation.

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:04.810
So a deployment looks like this.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:08.310
And if it goes, it goes past
those tests and it succeeds,

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:09.510
we'll see all the logs.

00:31:09.510 --> 00:31:11.880
We'll see information about
what container it built.

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:15.900
We'll see all that nice output
of what this build resulted in.

00:31:15.900 --> 00:31:18.420
And in this case the artifact
of this build is a container,

00:31:18.420 --> 00:31:19.680
but it doesn't have to be.

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:22.650
You can just use it to deploy
things or whatever you want.

00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:25.530
It absolutely does not
have to be a container.

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:27.720
But, like I said, if
I've made a mistake

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:30.560
and I have committed something
wrong or anyone on our team

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:33.060
has committed something wrong
and accidentally got something

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:35.220
into production, it's
pretty easy to roll back.

00:31:35.220 --> 00:31:35.720
Right?

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:39.960
In a traditional situation
you pull down the Git repo.

00:31:39.960 --> 00:31:40.950
Right?

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:45.690
You remember how to roll
back the latest commit, which

00:31:45.690 --> 00:31:47.405
I have no idea how to do.

00:31:47.405 --> 00:31:49.530
I would have to Google that
and be like, all right.

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:53.010
Something, something,
head hard reset something.

00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:56.220
And after probably like 10
minutes of messing around

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:58.680
I'd be able to figure out
how to go back a commit,

00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:01.170
and then I'd be able
to go rerun the scripts

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:02.740
and get into production.

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:05.610
But if you have a nice tool
like this, all of these builds

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:07.110
are really nice and contained.

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:08.550
So again, hard to
see, but there's

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.370
a very tiny button up at the
top that just says "rebuild."

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:14.160
And if I hit that rebuild
button, it's going to go.

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:17.730
It's going to go to the commit
that this build was built on,

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:20.820
rerun everything,
redo all the steps,

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:23.302
and at the end of the day
throw that build back up.

00:32:23.302 --> 00:32:25.260
And by "the end of the
day" I mean after, like,

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.910
30 seconds once
it's done building.

00:32:27.910 --> 00:32:30.573
So now, instead of panicking
and looking up Git logs

00:32:30.573 --> 00:32:32.740
and remembering how to do
this, we can just say, oh.

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:33.240
OK.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:33.960
Just roll back.

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:37.050
Just hit that button, and
we are now back that commit.

00:32:37.050 --> 00:32:39.984
We turn a few
minutes of downtime

00:32:39.984 --> 00:32:42.900
out of what could have been
half-hour or more of people

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:44.400
trying to remember
how to do things,

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:45.775
looking up obscure
documentation,

00:32:45.775 --> 00:32:47.050
things like that.

00:32:47.050 --> 00:32:48.380
So it's really, really helpful.

00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:57.300
I think one of the
things that CI/CD tools

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:00.360
are most valuable for is not
just deploying to production,

00:33:00.360 --> 00:33:01.720
because that's great.

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:04.833
But if we want people to
always be testing their code

00:33:04.833 --> 00:33:06.750
and always experimenting
and things like that,

00:33:06.750 --> 00:33:08.667
we really want to have
different environments.

00:33:08.667 --> 00:33:10.410
And this leads into
the next topic here,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:13.230
where you have configs
and environmental codes.

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:18.120
By "config" I mean things like
API keys, servers, anything

00:33:18.120 --> 00:33:21.450
that your service is
actually talking to.

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.040
These are all
configuration values.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:23.540
Right?

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:25.560
Any port, anything like that.

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:27.060
And then we have
environments, which

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:30.400
are kind of groups of
configuration information.

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:36.360
So if you have a service that's
talking to a MySQL database,

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.510
for example, and
you know that when

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:41.820
you deploy that
service to staging

00:33:41.820 --> 00:33:45.210
and you have to tell it to talk
to a different MySQL database,

00:33:45.210 --> 00:33:47.907
you have to run it with
the right config value.

00:33:47.907 --> 00:33:50.490
You have to deploy it to staging
with the right config values.

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:51.990
Otherwise what's
going to happen is,

00:33:51.990 --> 00:33:53.910
it's going to talk to
the production database

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:55.410
and very bad things
are going to happen.

00:33:55.410 --> 00:33:57.118
You're either going
to leak PII or you're

00:33:57.118 --> 00:33:58.870
going to accidentally
delete something

00:33:58.870 --> 00:34:01.480
you didn't mean to delete,
or anything like that.

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:03.270
And this is why we
get into this idea--

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.890
and this is a very
Firebasey idea.

00:34:04.890 --> 00:34:07.620
The Firebase knows where
your project is running.

00:34:07.620 --> 00:34:10.290
At anytime any code you're
writing with Firebase

00:34:10.290 --> 00:34:13.050
has an idea of what project
it's associated with.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:15.100
So this is true in
our client-side SDKs.

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:17.139
So if you're running
in the Android SDK,

00:34:17.139 --> 00:34:18.659
you can get that
project idea out

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:20.940
and you can say, all
right, what project ID

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:22.590
is my base configuration for?

00:34:22.590 --> 00:34:24.420
If you're writing
a Cloud Function,

00:34:24.420 --> 00:34:26.300
we have environmental
configuration values.

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:28.179
They're basically
environment variables,

00:34:28.179 --> 00:34:29.760
but made a little nicer.

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:33.010
And these know exactly
what environment you're in.

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:35.242
So if I was building a
service and I was working out

00:34:35.242 --> 00:34:36.909
these configuration
values I'm thinking,

00:34:36.909 --> 00:34:39.120
here's my user's
database, and here's

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:42.639
my whatever-whatever database.

00:34:42.639 --> 00:34:45.267
If I committed that
into Git, not only

00:34:45.267 --> 00:34:47.100
do I not have a log of
potentially-sensitive

00:34:47.100 --> 00:34:49.100
information, there could
be credentials in there

00:34:49.100 --> 00:34:51.960
or anything like that, but
we're also into a situation

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:53.985
where it's up to the
developer-- again,

00:34:53.985 --> 00:34:55.110
think of the new developer.

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:57.610
Think of someone who doesn't
have that much experience here.

00:34:57.610 --> 00:34:59.880
It's up to them
to know to switch

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:04.410
those with the right credentials
when they deploy into staging.

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:07.883
So another mantra, Git is
for code, not configuration.

00:35:07.883 --> 00:35:10.050
There are very few situations
where you should store

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:12.543
your configuration data in Git.

00:35:12.543 --> 00:35:14.460
It generally doesn't
work well, because if you

00:35:14.460 --> 00:35:17.670
leak a secret accidentally,
it's very hard to undo.

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.720
And if you have a situation
where these variables are

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:23.610
limited in scope,
you're now dealing

00:35:23.610 --> 00:35:26.190
with specific permissions
to a Git repo and, like,

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:27.910
only this person
should be able to read,

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:29.580
and this person can change them.

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:32.610
It's much, much easier to keep
all of that configuration data

00:35:32.610 --> 00:35:33.847
in the environment.

00:35:33.847 --> 00:35:36.180
So let me give you two examples
of what that looks like.

00:35:36.180 --> 00:35:38.490
Here is a web page--
a very simple web page

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:41.700
pulling in environmental
data from Firebase.

00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:44.730
So if you've ever deployed a
web app on Firebase Hosting,

00:35:44.730 --> 00:35:46.890
this is the gist
of what you can do.

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:48.270
And it looks basically the same.

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:49.907
We're pulling in the
two Firebase SDKs,

00:35:49.907 --> 00:35:51.240
we're ready to use Firebase SDK.

00:35:51.240 --> 00:35:51.990
That's great.

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:53.550
But we have that
bottom line there,

00:35:53.550 --> 00:35:57.150
that underscore underscore
firebase.init.js.

00:35:57.150 --> 00:36:00.360
And what that's doing is saying,
hey, initialize my Firebase app

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:03.330
on my JavaScript to
point to whatever project

00:36:03.330 --> 00:36:05.260
I'm currently running on.

00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:06.870
So if you're deploying
this locally,

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:09.857
it'll be whatever your
local project is set to use.

00:36:09.857 --> 00:36:11.440
If you're deploying
this to production

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:14.235
it will automatically pull
it from the environment.

00:36:17.030 --> 00:36:20.590
So this means you can now commit
this without any configuration

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:21.100
information.

00:36:21.100 --> 00:36:22.270
You commit it to Git.

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:23.762
And then when you
go to deploy it,

00:36:23.762 --> 00:36:25.970
it will automatically connect
to where you deploy it.

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:28.137
So now, if we're talking
about a staging environment

00:36:28.137 --> 00:36:30.100
and a production
environment or a development

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:32.650
environment or whatever, we're
not concerned about this.

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:35.830
We can deploy the exact
same code, no changes,

00:36:35.830 --> 00:36:37.420
and it'll be safe.

00:36:37.420 --> 00:36:40.090
Diving into this a little more,
here's another Cloud Function,

00:36:40.090 --> 00:36:43.870
some more lovely JavaScript
that I don't recommend using.

00:36:43.870 --> 00:36:48.110
And you can see here, we do
some simple onWrite for checking

00:36:48.110 --> 00:36:50.620
email, send some
service to that.

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:53.423
But we have this authorization
token down there.

00:36:53.423 --> 00:36:55.090
If you're not familiar
with that syntax,

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:58.120
that's just string interpolation
in modern JavaScript.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:36:59.970
The important part
is, functions.config

00:36:59.970 --> 00:37:01.640
.someservice.key.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:04.300
That's a key stored in
our environment that

00:37:04.300 --> 00:37:05.810
isn't checked into our code.

00:37:05.810 --> 00:37:08.230
It's not written down anywhere
except in Google Cloud

00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:09.830
where it's nice and safe.

00:37:09.830 --> 00:37:11.350
So when you go to
call this service,

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:14.560
there's no chance you're
going to call the production

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:15.060
credentials.

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:16.977
The thing I always think
about here is Stripe.

00:37:16.977 --> 00:37:18.940
If you're ever doing
Stripe payment processing,

00:37:18.940 --> 00:37:21.040
the likelihood that
you will use your test

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:23.150
key in your production
environment or vice versa

00:37:23.150 --> 00:37:23.950
is very high.

00:37:23.950 --> 00:37:25.960
So doing this
allows you to really

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.935
easily separate that and just
be like, my code is my code.

00:37:28.935 --> 00:37:30.560
My configuration is
in the environment,

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:32.310
and I have my two or
three projects set up

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:34.210
that are the ones we
deploy to and test with.

00:37:34.210 --> 00:37:36.670
And unless we allow someone
to accidentally deploy it

00:37:36.670 --> 00:37:39.190
to production, there's no
way they're accidentally

00:37:39.190 --> 00:37:42.440
charging on Stripe or
doing something like that.

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:42.940
So, yeah.

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:45.490
If your code has no
configuration and no idea

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:48.160
about the environment, or
it has no context of what

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:49.780
project to associate
with, it has

00:37:49.780 --> 00:37:52.960
no idea what's going on except
from what the environment tells

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:53.590
us.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:57.010
You write these really nice,
clean, independent bundles

00:37:57.010 --> 00:37:57.890
of code.

00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:00.450
It's a much easier,
much more [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:00.450 --> 00:38:02.950
bundle that you can then take
and deploy to different places

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:04.060
and ship round.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:07.057
It's extremely, extremely clean.

00:38:07.057 --> 00:38:08.140
But this isn't everything.

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:08.420
Right?

00:38:08.420 --> 00:38:10.300
We've been talking a
lot about writing code,

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:12.010
and we don't have that
much time so luckily

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:13.718
we're getting into
the last few sections.

00:38:13.718 --> 00:38:16.480
That once your code
is in the real world,

00:38:16.480 --> 00:38:18.100
you want to keep an eye on it.

00:38:18.100 --> 00:38:20.200
And any time we're
talking about keeping

00:38:20.200 --> 00:38:22.870
an eye on anything
in Google Cloud,

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:25.460
we're talking about Stackdriver.

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:27.550
You've probably used
Stackdriver or heard of it.

00:38:27.550 --> 00:38:29.710
It's an incredibly powerful
tool for just all sorts

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.750
of analysis of your code
and your performance

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:34.000
and all of these great things.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:36.460
It has all these metrics
of Google services.

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:37.810
Firebase is no exception.

00:38:37.810 --> 00:38:38.758
Throws off to it.

00:38:38.758 --> 00:38:40.300
If you ever wanted,
for example, more

00:38:40.300 --> 00:38:42.520
insight in your Firebase
database or your cloud

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:44.260
Firestore instance,
that information

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:45.190
is all in Stackdriver.

00:38:45.190 --> 00:38:48.750
You just got to go look at it.

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:52.950
But Stackdriver, like anything,
has a barrier to entry.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:53.460
Right?

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:55.290
And I think one of the
biggest themes of this talk

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:57.373
is that we want to make
things easy on developers.

00:38:57.373 --> 00:38:59.520
We want to make them do
as little as possible

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:03.060
in a way that helps us
build more confidently.

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:04.800
And so developers
are already used

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:08.080
to this idea, this system
of how we debug code.

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:09.907
And I would love to
tell you that I believe

00:39:09.907 --> 00:39:11.490
the fundamental idea
of debugging code

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:14.370
is like breakpoints and
good tooling, but it's not.

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:18.585
People console.log or printf
the hell out of their code.

00:39:18.585 --> 00:39:21.870
It doesn't matter who you
are, it's how we debug stuff.

00:39:21.870 --> 00:39:23.730
And that's fine.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:24.230
Right?

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:26.520
Like, it's great that we
have more advanced tools

00:39:26.520 --> 00:39:27.960
in Google Cloud
and in Stackdriver

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.780
that do crazy in-production
and breakpoints and stacks

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:31.990
and stuff like that.

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:34.557
But most developers
aren't going to use those.

00:39:34.557 --> 00:39:36.390
And they're especially
not going to use them

00:39:36.390 --> 00:39:39.280
if they're trying to ship
something by next week.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:40.780
So let's just make it better.

00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:41.280
Right?

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:43.890
Let's not change anything that
the developers are already

00:39:43.890 --> 00:39:44.700
doing.

00:39:44.700 --> 00:39:47.140
Let's just make the
situation better for them.

00:39:47.140 --> 00:39:50.930
So just by console logging
in a Cloud Function,

00:39:50.930 --> 00:39:52.680
if you normally go to
the Firebase console

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:55.350
and check it out you
see a fairly simple view

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:56.230
of this information.

00:39:56.230 --> 00:39:58.320
But if you head over
to Stackdriver Logging

00:39:58.320 --> 00:40:03.480
inside the Cloud console, you
will see a much nicer view

00:40:03.480 --> 00:40:05.620
of that same data.

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:07.320
So this is every log.

00:40:07.320 --> 00:40:10.200
It actually pulls logs from all
sorts of Google Cloud Services.

00:40:10.200 --> 00:40:12.510
It's not just Firebase.

00:40:12.510 --> 00:40:13.950
It's not just Cloud Functions.

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:17.130
You can get logs from Kubernetes
or Cloud Run or anything

00:40:17.130 --> 00:40:18.400
like that.

00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:20.550
But in this case, these
are just console logs

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:22.188
from our functions.

00:40:22.188 --> 00:40:24.480
And you can see it's broken
up into a few columns here.

00:40:24.480 --> 00:40:26.243
Left column, a
little colored icon

00:40:26.243 --> 00:40:27.660
to tell us what
type of log it is.

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:30.420
And after that we have what
time it was happened that.

00:40:30.420 --> 00:40:32.520
Next to that we have
the function name

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:33.690
that reported this log.

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:36.870
So we have two functions here,
request cool and fulfill cool.

00:40:36.870 --> 00:40:39.840
And then after that we
have a unique invocation

00:40:39.840 --> 00:40:43.140
ID that, one-to-one,
whatever-whatever, that's

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.940
the id of that invocation.

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:46.980
And then after that we
have whatever printed.

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:48.220
So this is great.

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:49.900
But it's better if
we can filter in.

00:40:49.900 --> 00:40:52.632
So if we click on one
of those nice numbers,

00:40:52.632 --> 00:40:55.090
it'll automatically write this
little query for us and say,

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:55.780
all right.

00:40:55.780 --> 00:40:58.180
Here's the log for the
invocation of that function--

00:40:58.180 --> 00:41:00.630
that specific one that
you just clicked on.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:02.130
And we can see the
entire lifecycle.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:03.600
And you can see
now, when it's not

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:06.720
in this entirely
big blob of logs,

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:09.360
that we have things like
how long the function took.

00:41:09.360 --> 00:41:12.120
We have an idea about what
went through and what happened.

00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:13.840
And this is without
changing console log.

00:41:13.840 --> 00:41:15.485
This is just still
console logging,

00:41:15.485 --> 00:41:17.610
but getting ingested into
stack Stackdriver Logging

00:41:17.610 --> 00:41:18.377
automatically.

00:41:18.377 --> 00:41:20.460
And this is already there
for your Cloud Functions

00:41:20.460 --> 00:41:21.335
you're already using.

00:41:21.335 --> 00:41:23.010
You just have to go click on it.

00:41:23.010 --> 00:41:23.890
So this is great.

00:41:23.890 --> 00:41:25.830
You can write all sorts
of custom queries.

00:41:25.830 --> 00:41:28.950
You can do all sorts
of different things

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:30.450
in terms of creating
custom metrics.

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:33.322
If you wanted alerts based on
when certain errors happened,

00:41:33.322 --> 00:41:35.280
if you want to just search
for the word "error"

00:41:35.280 --> 00:41:37.500
and say anytime that happens
we should alert someone.

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:38.490
You can do that.

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:42.490
But luckily you don't have to,
because we can do even better.

00:41:42.490 --> 00:41:44.790
So we want to give
developers the tools

00:41:44.790 --> 00:41:47.530
to debug without placing
a burden on them.

00:41:47.530 --> 00:41:51.630
And one of those burdens is
not just changing the code,

00:41:51.630 --> 00:41:53.970
but the other burden is
actually looking at these logs

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:54.720
and understanding that.

00:41:54.720 --> 00:41:55.220
Right?

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:57.690
Like, looking through
this is not trivial.

00:41:57.690 --> 00:41:59.170
That takes time.

00:41:59.170 --> 00:42:03.150
So why don't we give them
a nice, aggregate view

00:42:03.150 --> 00:42:05.400
of that data of those logs?

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:07.518
And let's do it without
any code changes.

00:42:07.518 --> 00:42:09.435
Well, that's that
Stackdriver Error Reporting.

00:42:09.435 --> 00:42:11.490
So Error Reporting--
again, it's just in there.

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:12.990
You just got to go
click the button,

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:15.270
please use Stackdriver
Error Reporting, and all

00:42:15.270 --> 00:42:17.460
your Cloud Functions,
all your Firebase stuff

00:42:17.460 --> 00:42:20.010
will get aggregated into these.

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:23.760
See, instead of a big, big log
I have a few instances of errors

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:25.230
that have happened in my code.

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:26.850
And we have a lot
of nice information

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.250
here, like how many
times it's happened,

00:42:29.250 --> 00:42:31.620
when it was most recently seen.

00:42:31.620 --> 00:42:34.050
Of course, if we click into
that, the full stack trace,

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:36.240
we get a histogram of
when it's been happening.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:38.550
We can link it to
internal bug tracking.

00:42:38.550 --> 00:42:39.720
We have all of this.

00:42:39.720 --> 00:42:43.290
And most importantly, we have
the ability to set up alerts

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:45.060
when new bugs occur.

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:47.920
So we don't have to ever
look at that log reactively.

00:42:47.920 --> 00:42:49.440
We don't have to
go think about it.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:50.710
We can just say, all right.

00:42:50.710 --> 00:42:53.910
If a new error occurs or
an old error happens again,

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:55.530
just shoot me an email.

00:42:55.530 --> 00:42:56.660
Hit up pager duty.

00:42:56.660 --> 00:42:57.160
Right?

00:42:57.160 --> 00:42:57.930
Call the on-call.

00:42:57.930 --> 00:42:59.400
Do whatever you
have to do in order

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:03.040
to get this information across
that something is going wrong.

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:05.710
And again, no code changes.

00:43:05.710 --> 00:43:07.240
So once we've done that--

00:43:07.240 --> 00:43:07.740
all right.

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:11.790
Once we are very aware of
all the new errors happening,

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:15.480
the realization we all have
is that really not everything

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:16.710
bad happening is an error.

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:17.210
Right?

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:18.720
Like, there are
situations you don't

00:43:18.720 --> 00:43:22.420
want to get into that aren't
errors but they're not good.

00:43:22.420 --> 00:43:23.160
So what do we do?

00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:25.110
How do we have that insight?

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:28.403
Well Stackdriver
has another thing.

00:43:28.403 --> 00:43:29.820
And this section
of Stackdriver is

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:32.220
where all of these metrics
from all these Google services

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:35.360
get poured into this one place.

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:37.590
And in this case we're looking--

00:43:37.590 --> 00:43:40.470
it's very hard to see, but we're
looking at the execution of two

00:43:40.470 --> 00:43:41.615
different Cloud Functions.

00:43:41.615 --> 00:43:42.990
There's a pink
line on the bottom

00:43:42.990 --> 00:43:44.310
and a blue line on the top.

00:43:44.310 --> 00:43:46.800
The blue line is
the front-end server

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:48.230
and the pink line
is the one that

00:43:48.230 --> 00:43:50.147
actually does the creation
of the content that

00:43:50.147 --> 00:43:51.210
gets served up.

00:43:51.210 --> 00:43:53.600
So you would expect
in all situations

00:43:53.600 --> 00:43:56.150
that that blue line be
bigger than that pink line,

00:43:56.150 --> 00:43:59.060
because at most the amount of
requests to create something

00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:02.030
can be as big as the amount
of requests to serve it.

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:04.820
So in this case, where that
pink line is super low,

00:44:04.820 --> 00:44:06.513
that's exactly what we'd expect.

00:44:06.513 --> 00:44:07.430
And we can go in here.

00:44:07.430 --> 00:44:08.930
We can look at all
sorts of metrics.

00:44:08.930 --> 00:44:12.980
We can look at CPU time and
memory use and aggregate data

00:44:12.980 --> 00:44:16.640
over years or days or hours,
we can create dashboards

00:44:16.640 --> 00:44:18.965
all from all this
Firebase information.

00:44:18.965 --> 00:44:20.840
And it's not just stopped
at Cloud Functions.

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:22.220
Like I said, we have databases.

00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:23.570
We have Cloud Storage.

00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:27.140
We have the most insight
available to you about Firebase

00:44:27.140 --> 00:44:30.350
all thrown into
Stackdriver metrics.

00:44:30.350 --> 00:44:31.800
So that's cool.

00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:33.838
But again, it's
not the same if we

00:44:33.838 --> 00:44:35.630
don't get-- if I have
to look at that thing

00:44:35.630 --> 00:44:37.402
and think about it,
that's not as good.

00:44:37.402 --> 00:44:39.110
So we can go in and
create custom alerts.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:41.090
For example, in this situation
I have a Cloud Function

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:42.230
that runs once a day.

00:44:42.230 --> 00:44:45.560
It goes and clears some caches,
does some other housekeeping.

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:47.720
I don't really care if
that function doesn't

00:44:47.720 --> 00:44:50.180
run on any given day,
but I do want to know

00:44:50.180 --> 00:44:51.330
if it's stopped running.

00:44:51.330 --> 00:44:54.320
So I set up this alert based on
a custom report in Stackdriver

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:56.930
Logging that says, hey, if this
function hasn't been invoked

00:44:56.930 --> 00:44:58.440
in about 48 hours, go ahead.

00:44:58.440 --> 00:44:59.120
Hit me up.

00:44:59.120 --> 00:45:01.010
Shoot me a message
and let me know

00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:02.670
if something's going wrong.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:04.312
This can tie into
pager duty or any

00:45:04.312 --> 00:45:06.020
of the other on-call
services so that you

00:45:06.020 --> 00:45:08.750
can know when something--
whatever it is--

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:11.480
is going wrong in your service.

00:45:11.480 --> 00:45:14.960
And, of course, because
these situations are nice,

00:45:14.960 --> 00:45:17.740
well-understood
alerts and conditions,

00:45:17.740 --> 00:45:19.490
if that situation gets
resolved you'll get

00:45:19.490 --> 00:45:20.855
told about that, too.

00:45:20.855 --> 00:45:22.230
So not only do
you get told, hey,

00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:23.897
that function didn't
run for some reason

00:45:23.897 --> 00:45:25.790
but you'll get told,
oh, now it did.

00:45:25.790 --> 00:45:27.470
Now you're all good.

00:45:27.470 --> 00:45:27.970
All right.

00:45:27.970 --> 00:45:28.660
Last section.

00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:30.410
Shortest section-- pricing.

00:45:30.410 --> 00:45:32.300
Pricing calculators
are great, but they're

00:45:32.300 --> 00:45:34.400
absolutely useless if
you have no information.

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:36.030
If you've ever sat
down and thought,

00:45:36.030 --> 00:45:37.160
man, I want to build this thing.

00:45:37.160 --> 00:45:38.368
We're going to do this thing.

00:45:38.368 --> 00:45:43.450
How much would it cost to
run 100 users on this server

00:45:43.450 --> 00:45:44.960
or 1,000 or 100,000?

00:45:44.960 --> 00:45:47.030
That's just about
impossible to tell.

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:48.860
You have no idea how
many megahertz hours

00:45:48.860 --> 00:45:49.693
you're going to use.

00:45:49.693 --> 00:45:52.130
You probably can't guess
how much RAM you need.

00:45:52.130 --> 00:45:54.930
Any of these things are
very, very difficult to tell.

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:58.730
So if only there was a
way some service that gave

00:45:58.730 --> 00:46:01.550
us incredibly-detailed
information about our service

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:05.370
so that we could put reasonable
numbers into these calculators.

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:06.620
And we just talked about that.

00:46:06.620 --> 00:46:07.850
That's Stackdriver.

00:46:07.850 --> 00:46:11.550
All of that information to
do forward-looking analysis

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:13.550
is available in Stackdriver,
and you can plop it

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:15.140
into your calculator.

00:46:15.140 --> 00:46:17.150
Second thing, Firebase
now has paid support.

00:46:17.150 --> 00:46:19.400
So all of this-- everything
I've talked to you about--

00:46:19.400 --> 00:46:22.410
you can get on the phone
now and talk to someone.

00:46:22.410 --> 00:46:25.400
If you are already a
GCP paid support member,

00:46:25.400 --> 00:46:27.170
we now have experts
staffed there

00:46:27.170 --> 00:46:30.150
to talk to you about Firebase,
answer technical questions.

00:46:30.150 --> 00:46:32.150
Anything about scaling
Firebase you need to know

00:46:32.150 --> 00:46:35.430
is now included in
GCP paid support.

00:46:35.430 --> 00:46:35.930
All right.

00:46:35.930 --> 00:46:36.650
So what did we cover?

00:46:36.650 --> 00:46:38.810
Code, testing, deployment,
rollback, environment,

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:40.160
monitoring, pricing.

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.750
That's pretty much what you
need to be thinking about.

00:46:41.750 --> 00:46:43.400
I appreciate you all
coming to the talk.

00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:44.608
I'll be around for questions.

00:46:44.608 --> 00:46:45.740
Thank you for coming.

00:46:45.740 --> 00:46:48.490
